e. In Nb steel, grain size has an extremely large effect on R_crit. The Hall-Petch relation correlates yield stress with ferrite grain size via σ_y = σ_0 + k_y d^{-1/2}. The ductile-to-brittle transition temperature is inversely proportional to d^{-1/2}; an increase in α-grain size raises DBTT. Fine Nb(C,N) precipitates prevent α-grain growth, and rolling over 1250°–850°C decreases grain size from ASTM No. –2 to 7 [Data: Entities (179, 64, 177, 178, 920); Relationships (1672, 613, 614, 92, 57)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Processing schedules and thermomechanical treatment control microstructure.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The standard two-stage process is a rough–hold–finish process in which a high-temperature delay can lead to rapid recrystallization and a coarse-grained structure. The three-stage rolling process is compared with the standard two-stage process, and at the end of rolling grain size is finer for the three-stage process than for the second stage. A proposed thermomechanical treatment after controlled rolling cools C-Mn and Nb steels below Ar1 and then heats them to ~900°C, producing a very fine-grained γ-structure [Data: Entities (1006, 1005, 1129, 1139, 1148); Relationships (2444, 2448, 2122, 2124, 2126, +more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Strengthening mechanisms involve internal stress components.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Equation (8) expresses yield stress as σ_y = σ_0 + k_y d^{-1/2} = (σ_lh + σ_sh + σ_dh + σ_ph) + k_y d^{-1/2}. Internal stress σ_0 is divided into lattice hardening, solid-solution hardening, dislocation hardening, and precipitation hardening. Dislocation hardening σ_dh is small in C-Mn steel owing to rapid progress of recovery, but severe in Nb steel; insufficient recovery raises σ_dh. Dislocation hardening occurs in the non-recovery region, and its contribution can increase the transition temperature if larger than separation [Data: Entities (807, 808, 811, 915, 913); Relationships (1587, 1589, 1637, 2481, 1881, +more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"α-phase behavior produces a fine α-grain structure.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"In the α phase, deformed α remains mainly in a recovered condition rather than recrystallizing because of strain-induced precipitation of Nb(C,N) in the α substructure. Precipitation in the α substructure leads to a fine α-grain structure, and stabilization of the α sub-boundary network makes recrystallization very sluggish. A fine α-grain structure can also be produced by transformation from deformed γ. V(C,N) strain-induced precipitation likewise stabilizes the α sub-boundary network [Data: Entities (619, 133, 109, 596, 597); Relationships (1183, 139, 1210, 1148, 1105)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Alloying and composition effects: C and V.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Carbon is present in Nb(C,N), V(C,N), plain C steel, C-Mn steel, and C-Mn-Nb steel. Reducing C content improves weldability and toughness, and a C content below 0.1% has been used with Nb additions and low finishing temperature. Differences in strain-induced precipitation potential between V and Nb affect recrystallization retardation. V(C,N) precipitates also stabilize the α sub-boundary network [Data: Entities (121, 597, 455, 350, 351); Relationships (151, 1105, 812, 2376)].\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_tokens": 9527, "completion_tokens": 6159, "total_tokens": 15686, "compute_duration_seconds": 27.244190454483032, "streaming_responses": 0, "retries": 0, "requests_with_retries": 0}}}